# Bench subject: qwen3.6 with a context large enough to hold the benchmark.
#
# Build:
#   ollama create qwen3.6-bench -f deploy/ollama/Modelfile.qwen3.6-bench
#
# Verify (must print 32768, not the architectural 262144):
#   curl -s http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/show -d '{"model":"qwen3.6-bench"}' \
#     | grep num_ctx
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# `ollama pull qwen3.6:35b-a3b-q4_K_M` gives a model whose architecture supports
# 262144 tokens but which carries NO num_ctx parameter. Ollama therefore serves
# it at its own small default. The read-only MCP surface is five tools plus their
# JSON schemas, and each diagnosis turn appends telemetry excerpts to a growing
# transcript; that overruns the default quickly. The model then drops the
# earliest tool output with no error and no warning, and the agent fails for
# reasons that have nothing to do with its diagnostic ability.
#
# A benchmark result produced under silent truncation is worse than no result,
# because it looks like a finding. `argus bench run` probes /api/show and aborts
# below --min-context (default 32768) rather than trusting this file was built.
FROM qwen3.6:35b-a3b-q4_K_M

PARAMETER num_ctx 32768

# Partial GPU offload: 28 of 40 layers on the card, the rest in system RAM.
#
# This model is ~22 GiB at Q4_K_M and does not fit a 16 GB card. Letting Ollama
# size the offload itself does not degrade gracefully — it over-commits and dies
# with "CUDA error: shared object initialization failed", taking llama-server
# with it (0xc0000409). That failure is NOT a Blackwell/driver incompatibility
# and NOT caused by num_ctx above: the same crash happens on the base model, and
# an explicit layer count works fine. Pinning the number is the whole fix.
#
# Set here rather than per request because Ollama's OpenAI-compatible /v1
# endpoint does not accept native options, and the bench agent speaks /v1.
#
# Measured on an RTX 5070 Ti (16.3 GB, driver 610.74) at num_ctx 32768, with the
# model unloaded between measurements — leaving a previous runner resident makes
# the lower settings look far worse than they are:
#
#   num_gpu     tok/s    note
#    0 (CPU)     2.4     baseline
#   20          22.7
#   26          46.1     <- chosen (mean of 2)
#   28          48.8     mean of 2; VRAM 16.0 of 16.3 GB
#   30           6.1     thrashing — reports no error, just collapses
#
# 28 is ~5% faster than 26 and leaves roughly 300 MB of VRAM free. 26 gives up
# that 5% for ~1.1 GB of headroom, which is the better trade for an unattended
# matrix: anything else claiming VRAM mid-run (a browser, Docker Desktop, the
# desktop compositor) tips it into the 30-layer regime, and that failure is
# silent — worse-than-CPU throughput with no error to alert you. Raise to 28 for
# attended runs on an otherwise idle machine.
#
# The full 32k KV cache is allocated up front, so these numbers already include
# it; context filling during a run will not push it over later.
PARAMETER num_gpu 26

# Sampling is deliberately left at the model's own recommended values
# (temperature 1, top_p 0.95, top_k 20, presence_penalty 1.5) rather than forced
# to greedy decoding. Qwen thinking models degrade into repetition loops at
# temperature 0, which would look like agent incompetence in the results. Run
# variance is handled the honest way instead — `--repeats N`, with the standard
# deviation reported next to every mean.
